  "body": "If you do\n\n``` js\nconst s = new ReadableStream({ highWaterMark: 1024 }, push => {\n  push(str2ab(\"123\"));\n  push(str2ab(\"456\"));\n});\n\ns.read().then(data => console.log(ab2str(data)));\n```\n\nshould this log \"123\" or \"123456\"?\n\nI am under the impression that at a lower I/O level, as data is pushed in, you accumulate it into a single growing buffer. Thus making it read \"123456\" is natural, as otherwise you would have to do bookkeeping to track how the array buffers were originally pushed in, and then slice them and even do some memory copying (or create copy-on-write array buffers to avoid that) to provide them to the `read()` caller.\n\nHowever, @isaacs mentioned that at least in some cases, Node.js does not do this, instead maintaining the pushed-in buffers separately.\n\nNot concatenating helps generalize the stream API beyond \"concatenable\" types, potentially to general objects. If it does not impose undue implementation costs, it is worth investigating.\n\nThis requires expertise from someone familiar with the low level I/O details of existing stream implementations and OS I/O primitives to comment intelligently on.\n",
